🎯 1. Why do we need Comparator / Comparable?
________________________________________________________________
👉 When you want to sort objects
Example:
Sort numbers ✅ easy
Sort custom objects (Student, Employee) ❌ not automatic
👉 So Java gives:
      Comparable
Comparator

🧠 2. Comparable (Natural Sorting)
👉 Used when class itself defines sorting rule
🔹 Method:
int compareTo(T obj);

🧠 Rule:
Return  |	Meaning
0	    |   equal
+ve 	|   greater
-ve	    |   smaller


🧠 3. Comparator (Custom Sorting)

👉 Used when:

You want multiple sorting logic

You don’t want to modify class

🔹 Method:
int compare(T o1, T o2);

🎯 4. Real-Life Example
👉 Employee sorting:
by salary
by name
by age
👉 Use Comparator (multiple sorting)


🧠 5. Comparator using Lambda (Java 8)
list.sort((a, b) -> a.name.compareTo(b.name));

🔥 6. Comparator using Method Reference
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(s -> s.name));

🚀 7. Advanced (VERY IMPORTANT)
Sort by multiple fields:
list.sort(Comparator
        .comparing((Student s) -> s.marks)
        .thenComparing(s -> s.name));


🎯 8. Comparable vs Comparator
Feature 	Comparable	        Comparator
Package	    java.lang	          java.util
Method	    compareTo()        	compare()
Sorting 	Single	            Multiple
Modify class	Yes 	        No
Java 8 support	No lambda	    Lambda supported

9. Where we use it?
👉 Used in:
Collections.sort()
List.sort()
TreeSet
TreeMap

"Comparable is used for natural sorting and allows only
 one sorting logic, while Comparator is used for custom
 sorting and allows multiple sorting logic. Comparator is more
flexible and supports lambda expressions in Java 8."